1
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:09,060
Twenty-five centuries ago,

2
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:10,980
a man named Zeno proved that

3
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:12,680
<i>nothing ever arrived.</i>

4
00:00:14,639 --> 00:00:16,679
Hi. I am Jahan,

5
00:00:16,719 --> 00:00:19,139
and something impossible
happened to me.

6
00:00:20,376 --> 00:00:21,626
<i>Her.</i>

7
00:00:28,486 --> 00:00:30,526
Zeno's argument is simple.

8
00:00:31,110 --> 00:00:32,630
To reach any place,

9
00:00:32,670 --> 00:00:34,810
you must first cover
half the distance.

10
00:00:35,324 --> 00:00:37,084
Then half of what remains.

11
00:00:37,618 --> 00:00:39,158
Then half of that.

12
00:00:39,848 --> 00:00:42,165
The halves never run out.

13
00:00:42,205 --> 00:00:44,145
There is always a remainder,

14
00:00:44,185 --> 00:00:46,445
and the remainder
has a remainder,

15
00:00:46,485 --> 00:00:48,625
so the traveller
is stranded forever

16
00:00:48,665 --> 00:00:50,965
in the generous
middle of the road.

17
00:00:52,877 --> 00:00:56,857
For centuries, this was treated
as a puzzle about motion.

18
00:00:57,317 --> 00:00:58,297
I submit it is

19
00:00:58,337 --> 00:00:59,997
<i>a puzzle about waiting.</i>

20
00:01:02,137 --> 00:01:03,557
Here is the evidence.

21
00:01:04,302 --> 00:01:05,262
A gate.

22
00:01:05,685 --> 00:01:06,685
A street.

23
00:01:07,171 --> 00:01:09,131
Eleven metres of tarmac.

24
00:01:09,630 --> 00:01:11,390
The hazard lights ticking.

25
00:01:13,532 --> 00:01:16,072
And you, still somewhere
in the first half,

26
00:01:16,112 --> 00:01:17,332
which makes you,

27
00:01:17,372 --> 00:01:20,472
by every known law
of logic, impossible.

28
00:01:21,792 --> 00:01:24,212
I have watched this
hundreds of times.

29
00:01:28,626 --> 00:01:31,706
Mathematicians eventually
rescued us.

30
00:01:32,075 --> 00:01:34,555
Add every half
together, they said:

31
00:01:34,595 --> 00:01:36,675
a half, a quarter, an eighth,

32
00:01:36,715 --> 00:01:38,835
and the total is exactly one.

33
00:01:40,035 --> 00:01:42,855
<i>The infinite fits</i>
<i>inside a finite walk.</i>

34
00:01:43,242 --> 00:01:44,502
How reassuring.

35
00:01:46,349 --> 00:01:49,103
But the sum only
measures how far.

36
00:01:49,143 --> 00:01:52,080
It cannot say what
the in-between is like:

37
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:54,900
the hair in your face
at the quarter mark,

38
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:58,580
the half-smile you wear
when you think nobody's watching.

39
00:02:03,453 --> 00:02:07,293
Heraclitus said no one steps
into the same river twice.

40
00:02:07,802 --> 00:02:11,022
Nobody gets into
the same car twice, either.

41
00:02:12,348 --> 00:02:15,408
<i>Hundred arrivals,</i>
<i>hundred different women,</i>

42
00:02:15,448 --> 00:02:17,488
and every single
one of them you.

43
00:02:29,264 --> 00:02:33,064
Diogenes answered Zeno
by standing up and walking.

44
00:02:33,459 --> 00:02:36,339
You answer him every time
by getting in.

45
00:02:40,684 --> 00:02:42,584
<i>Love is not the proof.</i>

46
00:02:45,491 --> 00:02:48,751
Love is the practical,
slightly awkward,

47
00:02:48,791 --> 00:02:51,231
repeated action
of putting the hazards on

48
00:02:51,271 --> 00:02:54,531
and sitting there
while she crosses the distance

49
00:02:54,571 --> 00:02:57,531
that logic says
can never be finished.

50
00:03:00,464 --> 00:03:01,714
<i>I am here.</i>

51
00:03:02,576 --> 00:03:06,992
<i>No hurry.</i>
